    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures how initialization choices affect loss across model sizes, but it does not test whether loss follows a power law, whether small-run fits extrapolate, or whether compute-optimal allocations follow a stable trend.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports loss changes from initialization, RMSNorm epsilon, and gated attention, plus downstream benchmark comparisons. These results do not directly test any listed claim version. It also reports attention-sink, token-level, residual-flow, and stable-rank analyses; those do not measure a listed version.",
    "test_reason": "The study varies initialization scale (γ), the RMSNorm ε and gated attention, and reports loss and benchmark differences at a few model sizes (dense 0.1B–1.5B; MoE 1.5B and 3B total). It fits no power law in parameters, tokens or compute. It runs no extrapolation or compute-optimal allocation test, and does not vary depth or width at fixed parameter count. It mentions a 'better scaling law with model size' and a ~79% effective-size gain, but reports no fitted exponent. So its results do not bear directly on V1–V7.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors say smaller initialization (γ=1) improves pretraining loss and downstream scores in dense and MoE models. Under standard RMSNorm ε and attention sinks the gain fades with size; reducing ε and adding gated attention restore it. γ>1 degrades training via residual-flow imbalance. Gains stem from a condensation (low-to-high complexity) trajectory and concentrate on context-dependent tokens.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study varies Gaussian initialization scale and architectural settings; it does not fit parameter-, token-, or compute-scaling laws or test compute-optimal allocation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported initialization benefit varies with model size, RMSNorm epsilon, gated attention, and dense versus MoE architecture.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Under standard RMSNorm ε and attention, the loss gain from small initialization shrinks with size (0.05 at 0.1B to 0.003 at 1.5B). With reduced ε and gated attention, authors say the gain persists and claim a 'markedly better scaling law with model size'.",
        "proposed_claim": "Initialization scale, interacting with normalization ε and attention gating, shifts the loss-versus-model-size trend of LLM pretraining.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R1",
          "R2",
          "R7",
          "R8",
          "R9",
          "R10",
          "R11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Final validation loss improves from γ=0.5 to γ=1 and worsens for γ>1.",
        "proposed_claim": "Pretraining loss has an optimum in initialization scale near γ=1 for pre-norm Transformers.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R12"
        ]
      }
    ],
